translations.text:
As for the imagination of stones, nearly every lavish account is unreliable:

from afar, their animal pregnancy is unquestionably different;
though contemporaries of the highest peaks, they do not come from stars;
their nature is not alchemical but musical;
few are doves, almost all are dancers, hence their charm;
whether disfigured or sealed, only their majesty communes with the Figure;
despite their stillness, they are not androgynous;
they breathe through lungs, and before becoming what they are, were machines of air;
books attest that among them are no Himalayas,
nor prostitutes;
they wear no cloak, and their only garment is their flayed skin;
they are more sea than the sea, and have wept;
even the largest fly in all directions at night and do not go mad;
they are born blind and see God;
airflow is their substance;
they have not read Wittgenstein, but know that he is wrong;
they do not bury their dead;
originality in the matter of roses disgusts them;
they do not believe in inspiration or eat fireflies,
nor do they believe in the farce of humor;
they like poetry provided it makes no sound;
they do no business with applause;
they turn seventy every second and laugh at fish;
test-tube babies make them yawn;
glorious armies seem wretched to them;
they hate aphorisms and bloodshed;
they are geometers and wear platinum rings in their ears;
they live on sacred leisure.
